; bl.mail.test
;
; A DNSBL fixture. A blacklist is queried by reversing the address octets and prefixing them to the
; zone: 192.0.2.21 becomes 21.2.0.192.bl.mail.test. A name IN the zone means listed; a name absent
; is NXDOMAIN and means clean, which is why a blacklist chain asserts expectedRcode 3 and passes
; when the address is not on the list.
;
; 127.0.0.2 is the conventional answer for "listed", and the TXT carries the reason, which is what
; the real lists do.

$ORIGIN bl.mail.test.
$TTL 60

@       IN  SOA  ns1.bl.mail.test. admin.bl.mail.test. (
                 2026092401 ; serial
                 3600       ; refresh
                 900        ; retry
                 604800     ; expire
                 60 )       ; minimum

@       IN  NS   ns1.bl.mail.test.
ns1     IN  A    127.0.0.1

; 192.0.2.21, which is mail.weak.mail.test. Listed.
21.2.0.192      IN A    127.0.0.2
21.2.0.192      IN TXT  "Listed: spam originating from this address, see https://bl.mail.test/why"

; 192.0.2.30, which is broken.mail.test. Listed for a different reason, so a chain can assert on
; the code as well as on presence.
30.2.0.192      IN A    127.0.0.3
30.2.0.192      IN TXT  "Listed: open relay"

; 192.0.2.11, which is mail.good.mail.test, is deliberately ABSENT. A query for it answers
; NXDOMAIN, which is what a clean address looks like and what the chain's passing case needs.
;
; Not fixture-able here: SERVFAIL, which is what a retired or refusing blacklist zone answers and
; which DNS_RCODE exists to tell apart from NXDOMAIN. CoreDNS has no way to return it for one name
; in a zone it is authoritative for. Pointing a query at a resolver that is down produces it.
